.TH SLEEP 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sleep \- suspend execution for an interval
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sleep
.I seconds
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sleep
suspends execution for
.I seconds
seconds by calling
.BR nanosleep (2),
then exits.
A caught signal that interrupts the sleep does not end it early.
.PP
.I seconds
is a non-negative decimal number with an optional fractional part, such
as
.BR 5 ,
.B 0.25
or
.BR .5 ;
digits beyond nanoseconds are ignored.
Anything else \(em a sign, a suffix such as
.BR s ,
or no digits at all \(em is rejected.
Arguments after the first are ignored.
.PP
There are no options.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The interval elapsed (or was 0).
.TP
.B 1
No operand was given (a usage message is written to standard output),
or
.I seconds
is invalid (a message is written to standard error).
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
$ sleep 5; echo done
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sh (1),
.BR time (1)
.SH BUGS
The usage message goes to standard output instead of standard error.
